s safe.\n\n**2. B: a3-d6** — attacks pawn d6, threatens discovered attack if Black moves pawn? But Black can capture with pawn e5xd6 possibly, or Queen captures. Opens up a3-h8 diagonal? Not very effective.\n\n**3. N: f3-d4** — blocks Black Queen’s view of d1? Actually Queen at g4 looks at d1 diagonal? g4 to d1 is diagonal a8-h1 direction? g4 to d1 is same diagonal? Wait a1-h8 diagonal: g4 is on a1-h8, so d1 is on same diagonal? No, d1 is not on same diagonal as g4. Actually g4 to d1 diagonal: g4(3,7) to d1(0,4)? Not same. Let’s not overcomplicate.\n\n**4. R: d1-d2** — connects rooks, unpins nothing. Seems passive.\n\n**5. P: d3-d4** — pawn break in center, attacks e5 pawn. Black’s e5 pawn just moved 2 squares so can capture en passant? Wait if White plays d4, Black’s pawn e5 can capture en passant if White’s pawn was on d3? Yes, en passant rule: if Black pawn moved two squares last turn from e7 to e5, and White has pawn on d3, if White plays d3-d4, then Black can capture en passant as if White pawn moved only one square. So d4 allows exd3 e.p.? Let’s check: Black pawn e5: PBT1 — flag T means eligible for en passant *for last move only*, so yes, Black just moved e7-e5 last turn. So playing d4 now would allow Black to capture en passant: e5xd6 e.p. But wait, en passant rule: only possible if capturing pawn is adjacent to enemy pawn after enemy’s two-square move. Black’s pawn is on e5, White pawn on d3, if White moves d3-d4, they are not adjacent before move, but after d4 they are adjacent? Actually before move: Black e5, White d3 not adjacent files (d and e are adjacent files). Yes they are adjacent files. So en passant is possible: Black can capture d4 with pawn e5, removing White’s d4 pawn and leaving Black pawn on d3. That loses a pawn for White. Not good.\n\n**6. P: b2-b4** — pawn push, gains space, maybe prepares Bc5 later. Safe.\n\n**7. K: g1-h1** — gets King off g-file where Black Queen is eyeing. King safer in corner? Possibly.\n\nGiven material disadvantage,